You are repairing an amplifier board that came back from the field. The board is simulated; treat it as a real one.

Schematic (common-emitter amplifier, NPN transistor Q1):
- supply: VCC = 15 V from node vcc to ground;
- bias divider: R1 = 68 kohm from vcc to b, R2 = 15 kohm from b to ground;
- collector resistor RC = 2.7 kohm from vcc to c; emitter resistor RE = 820 ohm from e to ground, bypassed by CE = 100 uF;
- input: signal generator at node in, coupled to the base by Cin = 10 uF;
- output: from the collector through Cout = 10 uF to node out, loaded by RL = 8.2 kohm to ground;
- Q1: collector c, base b, emitter e; a small-signal NPN, current gain around 150.
Parts are within 1 % of their marked values.

The board does not meet its spec. A good board has: collector DC voltage within 10 % of its design value, and a voltage gain from in to out at 1 kHz within 10 % of its design value. Exactly one part is faulty.

Tools: a multimeter for DC node voltages (to ground), a signal generator and scope for the gain at a frequency, an out-of-circuit test for any single part (it is lifted, tested and put back), and replacement of a part with a new good one. Replacing a good part is a mistake: each needless replacement halves the score. At most 40 tool calls.

Work like a repair technician: measure, work out what the bias should be, find the faulty part, confirm it, replace it, check the board, then call submit.
